      "id": 1,
      "text": "Mirando City sits on land once granted to Spanish rancher Nicolás Mirando, a fact that explains the town’s name.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mirando_City%2C_Texas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Before becoming a town, the area was a small ranching community that later grew into a bustling oil boom center.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/mirando-city-tx",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Oil pioneer Oliver Winfield Killam launched the South Texas Oil Boom in Mirando City in 1921, turning the town into a regional hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24,
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mirando_City%2C_Texas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "For over a century, local peyoteros have harvested peyote in Mirando City, a tradition that dates back more than a hundred years.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mirando_City%2C_Texas",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Mirando City lies roughly 30 miles east of Laredo and about 130 miles southwest of Corpus Chrism, placing it in eastern Webb County.",
